```scala
object Solution {
  def maxDistance(nums1: Array[Int], nums2: Array[Int]): Int = {
    var i = 0
    var j = 0
    var maxDistance = 0

    while (i < nums1.length && j < nums2.length) {
      // Move j to the right if nums1[i] <= nums2[j]
      while (j < nums2.length && nums1(i) <= nums2(j)) {
        j += 1
      }

      // Update maxDistance
      maxDistance = maxDistance.max(j - i)

      // Move i to the right
      i += 1
    }

    maxDistance
  }
}
```

This solution has a time complexity of O(n + m), where n and m are the lengths of the two arrays, respectively. It uses a constant amount of extra space to store the iterators and the maximum distance.

The code is structured to follow the flow of the problem, with the inner while loop advancing the iterator j to the right if the condition of a valid pair is met, and the outer while loop advancing the iterator i to the right, updating the maximum distance accordingly.

